WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.867 --> 00:00:10.200
COLLEEN BRIANT: Hi, I'm Colleen.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.380
DANA HOFFMAN: Hey, I'm Dana.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.660
Colleen and I are software
engineers at Google.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.214
We work on Google
Cloud Platform.

00:00:15.214 --> 00:00:16.630
And today, we're
going to show you

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.660
how to use the Cloud Foundry
service broker for Google Cloud

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.670
Platform that we
built in collaboration

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.720
with the engineers at Pivotal.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.770
So Colleen, you took the lead
building the service broker.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:29.841
Can you give us an overview
of what a service broker is?

00:00:29.841 --> 00:00:30.716
COLLEEN BRIANT: Sure.

00:00:30.716 --> 00:00:33.040
A service broker is a special
kind of Cloud Foundry app

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.010
that developers can use to
quickly and easily spin up

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.830
instances of different services,
and then get credentials

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.720
for their applications to use.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.880
It's integrated into
the Cloud Foundry CLI,

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:44.710
so developers can stay focused.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.050
And Cloud Foundry
operators can manage

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.420
the kinds of services and
plans that their developers

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:50.980
have access to.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.010
DANA HOFFMAN: Cool.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:55.210
So what's the quickest,
easiest way to actually get

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:56.909
a service broker installed?

00:00:56.909 --> 00:00:58.450
COLLEEN BRIANT: So
the service broker

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:01.730
is available as a tile
on network.Pivotal.io.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.150
So if you're using Pivotal
Ops Manager, the easiest way

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.339
to get up and running is
just to go that route.

00:01:06.339 --> 00:01:08.130
And normally this takes
about half an hour.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.770
But for the purposes
of this video,

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.010
I'm going to cut out
some of the waiting time.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:15.114
And all this is documented
on docs.Pivotal.io.

00:01:15.114 --> 00:01:16.780
But if you want to
follow along with me,

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.355
here are the steps you
would need to run through.

00:01:19.355 --> 00:01:20.980
To get started with
the service broker,

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.900
you need to create
a new GCP project

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.446
if you don't already have one.

00:01:24.446 --> 00:01:27.070
Then you'll need to create a new
service account for the broker

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:29.320
to use for all of its calls,
and give it owner level

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.760
access to your project.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.450
You'll need to enable the IAM
and cloud resource manager

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.570
APIs, as well as the APIs
for any other services you

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:38.939
want to enable.

00:01:38.939 --> 00:01:40.480
Then you'll need to
set up a database

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:41.920
to back the service broker.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.942
You can use any MySQL
database you want.

00:01:43.942 --> 00:01:45.400
But if you'd like
to use Cloud SQL,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.200
you can set up your
database in the console

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.520
using default settings.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.530
Except that you'll want
to enable network access

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:54.250
for Many IP, restrict
access to SSL connections,

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.650
and create a set of SSL
certs for the service broker

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:58.450
to connect with.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.540
After setting up your project
and database and enabling APIs,

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.900
you'll download the
tile from [INAUDIBLE].

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.444
Add it to your Ops
Manager installation.

00:02:07.444 --> 00:02:08.860
When you first add
it, you'll need

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.802
to go through some
configuration steps.

00:02:11.802 --> 00:02:13.510
First, you'll need to
add the credentials

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:17.745
for the service account you gave
owner access to your project.

00:02:17.745 --> 00:02:19.120
And then you'll
need to configure

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.100
access and credentials
for the database that

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.780
backs the service broker.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.580
Lastly, if you as
an operator want

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.921
to expose Cloud SQL
to your developers,

00:02:26.921 --> 00:02:28.420
you'll need to
configure some custom

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:33.204
plans based on machine type,
disk size, and pricing plan.

00:02:33.204 --> 00:02:34.870
Once you're all done
with configuration,

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:36.370
the tile will turn
green, and you'll

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.800
be able to apply your changes.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.410
When Ops Manager
finishes updating--

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:47.290
finishes updating--
finishes updating--

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:52.390
all of these services will be
available in the marketplace.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:54.540
Documentation for this
installation process,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.440
as well as for using
the service broker,

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:00.300
is available on docs.Pivotal.io.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.800
But if you're not
using Ops Manager,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.508
and you just wanted
to deploy the service

00:03:03.508 --> 00:03:05.790
broker as an app to your
Cloud Foundry installation,

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.560
it's publicly
available on GitHub.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.050
And instructions for installing
an app as a service broker

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:13.585
are available on
docs.CloudFoundry.org.

00:03:13.585 --> 00:03:15.960
COLLEEN BRIANT: And Dana, we
walked through this together

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.376
recently, and you were able
to build a super cool app that

00:03:18.376 --> 00:03:19.440
uses the service broker.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:20.880
Can you show us what you built?

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:21.755
COLLEEN BRIANT: Sure.

00:03:21.755 --> 00:03:23.820
And thank you, I think
it's pretty cool too.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.170
I built a [INAUDIBLE]
application

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:28.500
which scrapes Reddit
for cute animal photos,

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.660
classifies them using the
Google Cloud Vision API,

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:33.780
stores them in
Google Cloud Storage,

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.600
and displays the results.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.260
At this point, I've uploaded my
application to Cloud Foundry.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.830
But it won't run since it
has no valid credentials.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.740
To generate credentials and
create my Cloud Storage Bucket,

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.900
I used the service broker.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.480
The first step is to
create a storage service.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.240
I've named the storage bucket
[INAUDIBLE] Vision Bucket.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.020
And I'll refer to it with that
name from within the code.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.540
You can also let the service
broker generate a bucket name,

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.790
and have your code pull that
name from the environment.

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:05.850
Next, I'll bind it
to my application,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:08.610
and give the service account
admin permissions for my cloud

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:10.560
storage bucket.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.110
Now we have everything we
need to parse the credentials

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:15.210
from within our application.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.130
Restaging the app
takes a few minutes.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.260
So in the meantime, let's
take a look at the code.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.760
The credentials are stored in
the vCAB Services Environment

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:26.880
Variable.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.600
So our application parses
that variable as JSON

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.160
and stores it in a
Google credential

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.910
object, which can then be used
to access the APIs we need.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:40.770
Now that it's running,
let's take a look.

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:50.160
There's nothing in our
storage bucket yet.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.015
So the app is pretty empty.

00:04:52.015 --> 00:04:52.890
Let's run the scrape.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.940
And there we have it, cute
animal pictures with labels

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.130
generated from the
Google Cloud Vision API,

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:07.290
all served out of
Google Cloud Storage.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:09.270
COLLEEN BRIANT: Ah.

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:10.440
That's really cool.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.115
How long did it take
you to build that?

00:05:12.115 --> 00:05:14.490
DANA HOFFMAN: So when I first
started working on the app,

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.890
I was brand-new to
the service broker.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.810
But it still took
me just a few hours

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.660
to get it working within my
app, and to feel like I actually

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:24.600
understood what I was doing
and how the whole thing worked.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.220
Once I had it
working here, I was

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.080
able to use it in
another app later.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.020
And whole integration
took under an hour.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:31.020
COLLEEN BRIANT: Awesome.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:33.490
So do you have any
tips or best practices

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:35.490
for anybody else looking
to build an application

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:36.570
with a service broker?

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:37.470
DANA HOFFMAN: Sure.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.630
One best practice tip
that I'd like to mention

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.450
is using curated IM
roles for your bindings.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:46.110
These are rules that restrict
access to a single GCP service.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:48.990
As you saw earlier, when I bound
to my service instance created

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.900
through the service broker,
I passed a custom parameter,

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.270
which is the IM role that
controls what resources

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:56.400
the application has access to.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.080
For the sake of
security, we advise

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.450
using curated roles so
your application has

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.567
as few privileges as possible.

00:06:02.567 --> 00:06:04.650
You can see examples of
this in the service broker

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:07.606
documentation on
docs.Pivotal.io.

00:06:07.606 --> 00:06:09.480
And you can find out
more about curated roles

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:12.930
in the IM documentation
on cloud.Google.com.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:13.639
This is awesome.

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:15.180
Service broker's
really great, right?

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:15.660
COLLEEN BRIANT: Yeah.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:17.132
Service broker, best broker.

00:06:17.132 --> 00:06:18.215
DANA HOFFMAN: Best broker.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:27.970
COLLEEN BRIANT:
Thanks for watching.

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:30.550
Check out our playlist and
more videos on Cloud Foundry,

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:33.930
and make sure to subscribe
to the Google Cloud channel.

